# A DEBIAN MICROVM WITH NO DESKTOP: KERNEL, INITRAMFS, ROOT FILESYSTEM, SERIAL LOGIN.
#
# THIS IS THE THIRD OF THE THREE HEADLESS MICROVMS - Alpine, Debian, NixOS - and it is the one that
# took the longest to make boot, because Debian is the distribution whose kernel REFUSES the trick
# the other two get for free.
#
# A microvm with `pcie=off` has no PCI bus, so its disk arrives as virtio-over-MMIO. qemu offers to
# name that device on the kernel command line, and a kernel only accepts the offer if it was built
# with CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO_CMDLINE_DEVICES. MEASURED, out of each distribution's own `boot/config-*`:
#
#	Alpine 3.21   CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO=y   CMDLINE_DEVICES=y                  finds /dev/vda
#	NixOS 6.18    CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO=m   CMDLINE_DEVICES=y                  finds /dev/vda
#	Debian 6.12   CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO=m   # CMDLINE_DEVICES is not set        no /dev/vda
#	Void 6.12     CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO=m   (option not present)               no /dev/vda
#
# The engine answers that with `acpi=on` on the machine type, still with `pcie=off`, so qemu
# describes the same MMIO devices in a DSDT that every one of those kernels can read. That is why
# this file needs no distribution-specific flag and no patched kernel.
#
# NOTHING HERE INSTALLS DEBIAN. A microvm has no firmware to read a boot sector, so qemu is handed
# the kernel and the initramfs directly and the disk has to arrive holding a system. `.github/
# debian-rootfs.sh` builds the three files; this walkthrough boots them and reads the console.
#
# THE ASSERTION IS THE SERIAL LINE, NOT A SCREEN. There is no framebuffer on this machine - a GPU
# needs PCIe - so `microvm_display serial` is stated rather than assumed, and the recording is the
# "NO DISPLAY" card the recorder draws while a guest has no adapter.

walkthrough "debian microvm - a root filesystem, a serial login, and no PCIe"
  resolution 1024x768
  # THE VIDEO IS AN ARTIFACT EVEN WHEN THE GUEST DRAWS NOTHING, so its size is stated.
  recording_resolution 1024x768

  # THE THREE FILES THE CI STEP BUILDS, named here so the boot step can refer to them.
  needs debian-vmlinuz debian-initramfs debian.qcow2

  # THE ROOT FILESYSTEM, BUILT BY THE CI STEP AND NOT INSTALLED BY THIS FILE.
  disk debian.qcow2

  # `without a display` AND NOT `with a console`: this guest has no VGA adapter at all, which is the
  # honest shape for a machine with no PCIe. `with a console` would have the engine attach an ISA VGA
  # adapter that nothing ever draws on, and the recording would be a black field rather than a stated
  # absence. The phrase is part of the boot sentence - there is no separate key - which is why the
  # three shapes read as English: `with a desktop`, `with a console`, `without a display`.
  #
  # `rootfstype=ext4` IS NAMED BECAUSE THE INITRAMFS NEEDS TO AGREE WITH THE IMAGE. The disk is ext4
  # by construction; saying so keeps a future initramfs from probing for it, and `console=ttyS0` is
  # the only place this guest can be read.
  boot as a microvm from kernel "debian-vmlinuz" and initramfs "debian-initramfs" with "console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4" without a display with memory 1024, waiting up to 180s

  # THE FIRST FACT: the kernel found the disk. Without `acpi=on` this never appears, and the guest
  # instead reaches `ALERT! /dev/vda does not exist. Dropping to a shell!` after 30 seconds of
  # `Begin: Running /scripts/local-block ... done.` - which is what a wrong machine type looks like.
  the console will say "vda"

  # AND THE SECOND: init finished and a login is waiting. This is the fact the file exists to
  # establish - a distribution whose kernel refuses the microvm's default disk transport still
  # comes all the way up, because the transport it DOES accept is available.
  the console will say "login"

  # TWENTY SECONDS OF A GUEST AT A PROMPT, so the recording is a machine that stayed up rather than
  # a boot log that scrolled past.
  wait 20s

  stop the machine by killing it, waiting up to 60s
